# What never travels into a build context.
#
# From 2.9.0 the four builds take their context from the repository root
# rather than from each service's own directory, so that every image can copy
# the same LICENSE, NOTICE and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md — the notices have to
# ride inside a published image, and from ./app those three files were simply
# out of reach (see docs/FEATURES.md, "Installing by pulling").
#
# Moving the context up makes this file load-bearing rather than tidy:
# `songs/` alone is ~19 GB on this server, and Docker would try to send all of
# it to the daemon before running the first instruction. Without the two lines
# that exclude the library and node_modules, a build that used to take minutes
# does not finish. Patterns here are anchored at the root, exactly like the
# ones in .gitignore; `**/` is used where a directory can appear at any depth.

# The song library. The single reason this file must exist.
songs/

# Runtime data and secrets. `.env` holds USDB_PASSWORD, `ke-config` holds
# database.sqlite3 with password hashes and room invite codes; neither has any
# business in an image layer, and both sit right beside the Dockerfiles now.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
ke-config/
data/
backups/
pitch-cache/

# Scratch files and history. `.git` is 11 MB of no use to any build stage.
.git
.gitignore
tmp/

# Build output and installed dependencies: every image installs its own, and a
# host copy is both large and the wrong architecture.
**/node_modules
app/build
**/bin
**/obj

# Editor and tooling leftovers.
**/*.log
**/*.tmp
**/*.swp
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.sqlite3-shm
**/*.sqlite3-wal
**/*.tsbuildinfo
.vscode
.github
.DS_Store
